# LedSim

> **A modern 3D LED display simulation and visualization engine.**

LedSim is an open-source C++ application for simulating LED display panels in a realistic 3D environment. It is designed for engineers, developers, and hobbyists who want to develop, test, and visualize LED display systems before deploying them to physical hardware.

Unlike a simple pixel viewer, LedSim treats LED displays as real-world objects. Panels can be positioned, rotated, viewed from different angles, and eventually assembled into complete display walls.

---

# Why LedSim?

Developing software for LED displays is often difficult without physical hardware.

LedSim aims to solve that problem by providing a virtual environment where developers can:

- Preview graphics and animations
- Test rendering algorithms
- Simulate multiple LED panels
- Verify panel layouts
- Prototype display controllers
- Develop display firmware without hardware

The long-term goal is to become a complete LED display development platform.

---

# Planned Features

## Rendering

- Modern OpenGL renderer
- Hardware-accelerated GPU rendering
- GPU instancing
- Perspective and orthographic cameras
- Physically positioned LED panels
- Multiple camera views

## Display Simulation

- LED framebuffer
- Multiple panel layouts
- Various LED pitches (P2, P2.5, P4, P5, P10, etc.)
- Indoor and outdoor panels
- Cabinet simulation
- Brightness control
- Color correction
- Gamma correction

## Development Tools

- Test pattern generator
- Text rendering
- Image rendering
- Animation playback
- Video playback
- Panel configuration editor

## Future Hardware Support

- LED controller simulation
- Network protocol simulation
- Virtual commissioning
- Hardware debugging
- Firmware development support

---

# Design Philosophy

LedSim is built around one simple principle:

> **Display logic and rendering must remain completely independent.**

The LED framebuffer should know nothing about OpenGL.

Likewise, the renderer should know nothing about LED controller protocols.

```
Application
      │
      ▼
 Display Layer
      │
      ▼
 Rendering Layer
      │
      ▼
      GPU
```

This separation makes the system easier to maintain, extend, and reuse.

---

# Architecture

```
LedSim
│
├── Core
│   ├── Application
│   ├── Window
│   └── Input
│
├── Scene
│   ├── Camera
│   ├── Transform
│   └── Entity
│
├── Display
│   ├── Panel
│   ├── Pixel
│   ├── FrameBuffer
│   └── PanelConfiguration
│
├── Renderer
│   ├── Renderer
│   ├── Shader
│   ├── Mesh
│   ├── VertexArray
│   ├── VertexBuffer
│   ├── IndexBuffer
│   └── InstanceBuffer
│
├── Assets
│
└── External
```

---

# Rendering Pipeline

```
Display Data
      │
      ▼
FrameBuffer
      │
      ▼
Renderer
      │
      ▼
Mesh + Instance Data
      │
      ▼
OpenGL
      │
      ▼
GPU
```

A single LED mesh is reused for every LED using GPU instancing.

For example, a **64 × 32** panel contains **2,048 LEDs**, yet can be rendered using:

- One LED mesh
- One instance buffer
- One draw call

This approach scales efficiently to very large LED walls.

---

# 3D Scene

Unlike traditional LED simulators, every panel exists as an object in 3D space.

Each panel has its own:

- Position
- Rotation
- Scale

This makes it possible to simulate:

- Wall-mounted displays
- Angled installations
- Multi-panel arrangements
- LED cabinets
- Curved displays (future)

---

